===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|porfire|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} [[πορφυρίτες]] [[λίθος]] ("purple stone").

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈpɔrfɪri/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|porphyries}}

# {{context|geology|lang=en}} a hard [[igneous]] [[rock]] consisting of large [[crystal]]s in a [[fine]]-[[grained]] [[matrix]]
## A purple-red rock of this kind.

===Adjective===
{{en-adjective|porphyrous|sup=most porphyrous}}

# {{context|ornamentation|lang=en}} of or crafted from '''porphyry'''
#* {{quote-book|year=1980|author={{w|Colin Thubron}}|title=Seafarers: The Venetians|page=43|passage=A quartet of '''porphyry''' warriors, locked for all time in stern embrace outside {{w|St. Mark's Basilica}}, were probably carved in the Fourth Century. They are believed to represent the Roman Emperor {{w|Diocletian}} and the three men who held power with him.}}


